<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.4.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00117</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.4.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8b01a329-e348-4c00-9001-b2dedfec1712</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="4a72a149-d379-499f-8c98-c653e18ef3c5" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
  <!--Q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="14dbcfa0-6fce-471d-b730-09851a44d955" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Is \(y = \frac{4}{3}\) a solution of \(9y + 2 = 6y + 3\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Work smarter, not harder! Revisit activity 1.4.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="97304cdc-436a-4375-81ad-81c65c413a95" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Yes" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Is \(y = \frac{7}{5}\) a solution of \(5y + 3 = 10y - 4\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a747c5fd-0e3d-455c-abe5-6bde161905d2" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Yes" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Is \(y = \frac{5}{3}\) a solution of \(6y + 10 = 12y\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Work smarter, not harder! Revisit activity 1.4.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="3a00f8ce-d359-44d8-837b-1e3a937ded6e" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="No" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Is \(u = -\frac{1}{2}\) a solution of \(8u - 1 = 6u\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="b3c93ee1-8ba1-43d0-be55-003676986945" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="6">
    <problem>
      <para> Tickets to the museum cost $4.50 for children and $9.00 for adults. The equation \(63 = 4.5c + 9a\) represents
          the total cost for \(c\) children’s tickets and \(a\) adult tickets. How many children’s tickets were
          sold if 4 adult tickets were sold?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="79fbb396-0500-4dfd-9527-88a0842b3ef1" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(c = 12.5n-10\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(c = 12.5n-10\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(c = 10n-12.5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(c = 12.5(n-10)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(c = 12.5(10n)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>For questions 6 and 7, use the following scenario: T-shirts are on sale for $12.50. Isla has a coupon for $10.00 off. </para>
      <para> Choose the equation that represents the total cost,c, if she buys n t-shirts.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="3cc310a3-9e72-4995-907d-60606fc94d33" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="4">
    <problem>
      <para>How many T-shirts can she buy if she spends all of her $40?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="9eca9bca-6517-4bb7-b15a-6bc5daf1e385" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;2 necklaces and 5 bracelets&quot;, &quot;4 necklaces and 2 bracelets&quot;, &quot;No necklaces and 8 bracelets&quot; ]" data-solution-options="[&quot;5 necklaces and 1 bracelet&quot;, &quot;2 necklaces and 5 bracelets&quot;, &quot;3 necklaces and 3 bracelet&quot;, &quot;4 necklaces and 2 bracelets&quot;, &quot;3 necklaces and 5 bracelets&quot;, &quot;6 necklaces and no bracelets&quot;, &quot;No necklaces and 8 bracelets&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>An artist is selling children's crafts. Necklaces cost $2.25 each, and bracelets cost $1.50 per each. Select
          <emphasis>three</emphasis> combinations of necklaces and bracelets that the artist could sell for exactly $12.00
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q10-->
  <exercise data-content-id="55d7874c-c528-49c9-a123-a73eb0d5dd97" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;c=14 and v=4  and  are a pair of solutions to the equation.&quot;, &quot;If 6 cars go and 11 vans go, there will be extra space.&quot;, &quot;If 20 cars go, no vans are needed.&quot;, &quot;8 vans and 8 cars are numbers that meet the constraints in this situation.&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;If 12 cars go, then 2 vans are needed.&quot;, &quot;c=14 and v=4  and  are a pair of solutions to the equation.&quot;, &quot;If 6 cars go and 11 vans go, there will be extra space.&quot;, &quot;10 cars and 8 vans isn’t enough to transport all the students.&quot;, &quot;If 20 cars go, no vans are needed.&quot;, &quot;8 vans and 8 cars are numbers that meet the constraints in this situation.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Volunteer drivers are needed to bring 80 students to the championship baseball game. Drivers either have cars,
          which can seat 4 students, or vans, which can seat 6 students. The equation \(4c+6v=80\) describes the
          relationship between the number of cars, \(c\), and number of vans, \(v\), that can transport exactly 80 students.
          Select <emphasis>four</emphasis> statements that are true about the situation.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q11-->
  <exercise data-content-id="d460c6a6-e642-4be8-b480-b5e6c6985408" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="7">
    <problem>
      <para>Use the following scenario for questions 11 and 12:</para>
        <para>The drama club is printing T-shirts for its members. The printing company charges a certain amount for each T-shirt
          plus a setup fee of $40. There are 21 students in the drama club.
        </para>
        <para> If there are 21 students in the club and the T-shirt order costs a total of $187, how much does each T-shirt
          cost?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q12-->
  <exercise data-content-id="9fddb6dc-98b6-4911-ba3a-d1ee7c8ebe24" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="65">
    <problem>
      <para> The equation \(201.50=f+6.50(21)\) represents the cost of printing the T-shirts at a second printing company.
          Find the solution to the equation.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.4.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
